course: dictionary
lesson: 314
title: The tiny word and
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: «And» is a small word with a big job: it joins things. «Bread and butter» joins two things, and «cars and railings by the sea rust quickly» joins two things that rust for the same reason.
explain: «And» can also mean then or after that, as in «she smiled and waved», where the smiling comes first and the waving comes next. In sums it means plus: «two and three make five».
explain: Be careful with casual talk. «And?» on its own means «so what, why does it matter?», and it can sound rude. A formal writer uses «in addition to», «together with» or «furthermore» instead of a long string of ands.
fact: «And» is a conjunction: it joins two words or two parts of a sentence.
fact: «And» can mean then or after that: «she smiled and waved».
fact: In sums «and» can mean plus: «two and three make five».
fact: «And?» on its own means «so what?» and can sound rude.
fact: Formal writing uses «as well as», «in addition to», «together with» and «furthermore» to join ideas.
exercise: «Burning coal, oil and gas adds carbon dioxide to the air.» What does the word and join here? | Carbon dioxide and a blanket | Two different days | *The last two fuels in the list, oil and gas | Coal and the air
exercise: «She smiled and waved.» Which happens first? | *She smiled first, then she waved | She waved first, then she smiled | They happen at the same time, in a sum | Neither happens
exercise: In a sum, «two and three make five» means what? | Two times three makes five | Two sits next to three in a list | *Two plus three makes five | Two minus three makes five
test: In «Chloe and Bella are still frenemies in Year 6», what does and join? | Two parts of a sum | Two years of school | *Two girls' names, Chloe and Bella | The word frenemies and Year 6
test: «Furthermore» is a formal word. What kind of join does it make? | Makes the sentence a question | Stops the sentence completely | *It adds one more thing, like and | It only works in sums
exam: Jayden says «And?» to his friend after she tells him her news. What does he mean? | Please tell me more about it | I am happy for you and then I'll leave | *So what, why does it matter? | And that is the end of the story
